-- title:
--   Reducedness of B/mathfrak pB from a locally principal finite overring
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ and $B$ be commutative local rings with $B$ Noetherian, and let $B$ be an $A$-algebra that is flat as an $A$-module. Assume the ideal generated by the image of the maximal ideal of $A$ in $B$, namely the pushforward of $\mathfrak m_A$ along $A \to B$, equals the maximal ideal of $B$, and assume that for every $b \in B$ there is $a \in A$ with $b - \mathrm{alg}_{A,B}(a) \in \mathfrak m_B$ (so the residue map $A/\mathfrak m_A \to B/\mathfrak m_B$ is surjective). Let $D_1$ be a commutative integral domain which is an $A$-algebra, finite as an $A$-module, and suppose every maximal ideal $\mathfrak n$ of $D_1$ is locally principal in the following elementwise sense: there exists $t \in \mathfrak n$ such that for every $x \in \mathfrak n$ there are $s \in D_1 \setminus \mathfrak n$ and $y \in D_1$ with $sx = ty$. Finally let $\mathfrak p$ be an ideal of $A$ which is exactly the kernel of the structure map $A \to D_1$. Then the quotient ring $B \,/\, \mathfrak p B$, where $\mathfrak p B$ denotes the ideal of $B$ generated by the image of $\mathfrak p$, is reduced, i.e. has no nonzero nilpotent elements.
--
--   This is the classical criterion for analytic unramifiedness of $A/\mathfrak p$: if that domain admits a module-finite overring all of whose maximal ideals are locally principal (for instance a finite Dedekind overring, such as the integral closure when it is finite), then passing to a flat local $B$ with the same residue field and $\mathfrak m_A B = \mathfrak m_B$ — typically the $\mathfrak m_A$-adic completion — preserves reducedness. It is used in the construction and analysis of node annuli, where reducedness of quotients of a complete local ring by extended primes is needed.
